 "verdicts": {
  "ruling_decisive_read": "At 1.2 pct (0.41 ATR) ZERO of the 5 real winners are stopped and 5 of 6 TSM-harvest survivors remain. By the ruling own criterion the tight stop is DEFENSIBLE - it does not destroy the only exit path that ever paid.",
  "but_expectancy": "At the calibrated overshoot, expectancy is -0.436R at 1.2 pct versus -0.214R as traded. The tight stop roughly DOUBLES the loss rate per trade. Wider is monotonically better across 1.0-1.7 pct and better still as traded.",
  "metric_conflict": "The ruling kill metric (>=3R exits) moves the OPPOSITE way: 4 at 1.2 pct versus 0 as traded, because R = move/width mechanically manufactures big R at tight stops. Optimising the kill metric and optimising expectancy disagree on this parameter.",
  "harvest_floor": "The width at which the harvest actually breaks is near 0.8 pct, not 1.2 pct. At 0.6 pct one winner dies and harvest survivors fall from 5 to 3.",
  "atr_floor": "max(1.2 pct, k x ATR14) for k in 0.2-0.5 does not beat the flat 1.2 pct cap on this sample.",
  "overarching": "Expectancy is NEGATIVE at every width tested, from 0.8 pct to as-traded. No stop width makes this strategy profitable. This is the ruling third precommitted trigger: the stop was never the binding constraint, and the problem is signal quality - which is where gov 195/196/197 already pointed."
 }
